--TEST--
`Core\Script::args()` is the read half of `spawn script … with(args: …)`: a child sees the value its parent passed, and a child spawned without the option sees `null` — as does the root script, which nothing spawned — `rule:core-classes/script-args`, ADR 0006 § *Decision*
--FILE nvs.toml--
# ADR 0118 § 2: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no
# configuration cannot spawn at all. This grants it and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE reader.nvs--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/script-args`: the child asks for its argument rather than finding it in an
// ambient `$_ARGS`. The answer is typed `mixed`, which is why a plain string
// is a legal thing to have been passed — `args:` narrows nothing at the call
// site, so nothing narrower would be true here.
var $a = Core\Script::args();
echo "child got ", $a as string, "\n";
return "read";
--FILE quiet.nvs--
<?nvs
// The same read in a child spawned with no `args:` at all. The lowering hands
// `null` here rather than an empty array, because a parent that wrote
// `args: []` said something this one did not.
echo "quiet got ", Core\Script::args() == null ? "null" : "a value", "\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The root script was not spawned by anything, so there is no argument to
// read — the same `null`, reached by the same absence.
echo "root got ", Core\Script::args() == null ? "null" : "a value", "\n";

// The value crossed once, at the spawn (ADR 0006 § *Decision*). Reading it in
// the child is a read of the child's own arena and copies nothing further.
var $passed = spawn script "reader.nvs" with(args: "alpha", output: "capture");
var $done = await $passed;
echo "ok=", $done->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $done->output;

var $none = spawn script "quiet.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $empty = await $none;
echo "ok=", $empty->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $empty->output;
--EXPECT--
root got null
ok=true
child got alpha
ok=true
quiet got null
